You are resolving specific OPEN AMBIGUITIES about the C++ URL parser binary /workspace/dataset/test18_executable (18 output lines: scheme, authority, userinfo, host, port, path, query, fragment, eight has_* flags, to_string, is_valid). Use /tmp/probe.sh <url> or run the binary directly.

Context — rules gathered so far:
[
 {
  "family": "Scheme detection and validation",
  "probe_count": 125,
  "rules": [
   {
    "rule": "The scheme delimiter is the literal 3-byte sequence \"://\". A scheme/authority split is attempted ONLY if that exact substring occurs; a bare ':' with one slash or no slash never triggers it. Evidence: \"http:/x\" and \"http:x\" and \"a:/:/x\" all yield scheme \"\", has_scheme 0, and the ENTIRE input as path().",
    "confidence": "certain"
   },
   {
    "rule": "There is NO character-set validation of the scheme at all. Every byte before the delimiter is accepted verbatim, including '/', ':', '@', '\\\\', '%', space, tab, newline, digits, leading '+', '-', '.', and non-ASCII UTF-8 bytes. Confirmed schemes: \"a/b\" (a/b://c), \"///\" (///://x), \"/\" (/://x), \"a:b:c\" (a:b:c://d), \"a::\" (a::://b), \"1http\", \"ht_tp\", \"ht tp\", \"ht\\ttp\", \"a\\\\b\", \"a@b\", \"a%20b\", \"-a\", \"+a\", \".a\", \"0\", \"h+t-t.p\", \"ÄÖÜ\". None of these are rejected or downgraded to path.",
    "confidence": "certain"
   },
   {
    "rule": "The ONLY thing that suppresses scheme recognition (besides the delimiter being absent) is a '?' or '#' occurring earlier in the input than the first \"://\". Implement as: split fragment off at the FIRST '#' of the whole input; then split query off at the FIRST '?' of the pre-fragment part; then search for \"://\" only in the remaining leading segment. Evidence: \"ht?tp://x\" -> no scheme, path \"ht\", query \"tp://x\"; \"ht#tp://x\" -> no scheme, path \"ht\", fragment \"tp://x\"; \"?://x\" -> query \"://x\"; \"#://x\" -> fragment \"://x\"; \"a?b#c://d\" -> path \"a\", query \"b\", fragment \"c://d\"; \"a#b?c://d\" -> path \"a\", no query, fragment \"b?c://d\".",
    "confidence": "certain"
   },
   {
    "rule": "When \"://\" does occur before any '?' or '#', the FIRST occurrence wins; later occurrences are ordinary text. Evidence: \"http://a://b\" -> scheme \"http\", host \"a\", path \"//b\"; \"a://b://c://d\" -> scheme \"a\", host \"b\", path \"//c://d\"; \"AB://CD://EF\" -> scheme \"ab\", host \"CD\", path \"//EF\".",
    "confidence": "certain"
   },
   {
    "rule": "The search is a plain substring find, so overlapping colons shift the split: in \"a:://b\" the first \"://\" starts at index 2, giving scheme \"a:\" and host \"b\". In \"a:://:b\" scheme is \"a:\" and the remainder \":b\" is the authority (empty host, port \"b\" unparseable -> absent).",
    "confidence": "certain"
   },
   {
    "rule": "scheme() is lowercased byte-by-byte with ASCII tolower only: 'A'-'Z' -> 'a'-'z', every other byte (including UTF-8 continuation/high bytes) is left untouched. Evidence: \"HTTPÄ://x\" -> scheme bytes h t t p 0xC3 0x84 (verified with od -c); \"HtTp://x\" -> \"http\"; \"A+B-C.D://x\" -> \"a+b-c.d\". Host, path, query and fragment keep their original case (\"Http://EXAMPLE.com/PaTh?QuEry#FrAg\" -> host \"EXAMPLE.com\", path \"/PaTh\", query \"QuEry\", fragment \"FrAg\").",
    "confidence": "certain"
   },
   {
    "rule": "An input beginning with \"://\" yields an EMPTY scheme (has_scheme 0) but the authority IS still parsed from the text after the delimiter. Evidence: \"://x\" -> scheme \"\", has_scheme 0, authority \"x\", host \"x\", has_authority 1, has_host 1; \"://u@h:1/p?q#f\" -> userinfo \"u\", host \"h\", port 1, path \"/p\", query \"q\", fragment \"f\", has_scheme 0.",
    "confidence": "certain"
   },
   {
    "rule": "to_string() emits the \"scheme://\" + authority() prefix ONLY when has_scheme() is true. If the scheme is empty but an authority was parsed, the authority (and userinfo/host/port) is silently DROPPED from to_string(), making it lossy. Evidence: \"://x\" -> to_string \"\" (verified byte-exact empty line via od); \"://x:80\" -> \"\"; \"://u@h:1/p?q#f\" -> \"/p?q#f\"; \"://a?b://c\" -> \"?b://c\"; \"://a/b?c#d\" -> \"/b?c#d\".",
    "confidence": "certain"
   },
   {
    "rule": "Full to_string() formula consistent with every probe: (has_scheme() ? scheme() + \"://\" + authority() : \"\") + path() + (has_query() ? \"?\" + query() : \"\") + (has_fragment() ? \"#\" + fragment() : \"\"). Note authority() is the RECONSTRUCTED string and is emitted even when has_authority() is 0. Evidence: \"http:////x\" -> \"http://\"+\"\"+\"//x\" = \"http:////x\"; \"a:://:b\" -> \"a:\"+\"://\"+\"\"+\"\" = \"a:://\"; \"http://:8080\" -> \"http://:8080\" (authority \":8080\" though has_authority 0); \"a://?\" -> \"a://\" (empty query delimiter dropped).",
    "confidence": "certain"
   },
   {
    "rule": "After a recognized \"://\", the authority runs to the first '/' in the remaining (already query/fragment-stripped) segment; that '/' and everything after it is the path. If the delimiter is immediately followed by '/', the authority is empty. Evidence: \"a:///b\" -> scheme a, authority \"\", has_authority 0, path \"/b\"; \"http:////x\" -> path \"//x\"; \"a://///b\" -> path \"///b\"; \":///x\" -> no scheme, no authority, path \"/x\", to_string \"/x\".",
    "confidence": "certain"
   },
   {
    "rule": "Because query/fragment are split before authority parsing, '?' and '#' also terminate the authority. Evidence: \"http://a#b:80\" -> host \"a\", port -1, fragment \"b:80\" (NOT host \"a#b\" port 80); \"http://a#b/c\" -> host \"a\", path \"\", fragment \"b/c\"; \"http://a?b/c\" -> host \"a\", path \"\", query \"b/c\"; \"http://a:8#0\" -> port 8, fragment \"0\".",
    "confidence": "certain"
   },
   {
    "rule": "No whitespace is ever trimmed, at any position. Leading/trailing spaces become part of the scheme and are reproduced by to_string(). Evidence: \" http://x\" -> scheme \" http\", to_string \" http://x\"; \"http ://x\" -> scheme \"http \"; \"a  ://x\" -> scheme \"a  \"; \" ://x\" -> scheme \" \" (has_scheme 1!); \"  ://x\" -> scheme \"  \"; \"\\t://x\" -> scheme \"\\t\"; \"s:// ?q\" -> host \" \", has_host 1, is_valid 1.",
    "confidence": "certain"
   },
   {
    "rule": "has_scheme() is exactly scheme().empty() == false. The only way to get an empty-but-present delimiter is the input starting with \"://\" (or the pre-query segment starting with it). A whitespace-only scheme still sets has_scheme 1.",
    "confidence": "certain"
   },
   {
    "rule": "An 'invalid-looking' scheme never causes a fallback re-parse: there is no code path that turns a bad scheme into a path. Either \"://\" is found in the leading segment (scheme + authority parsed, whatever the scheme text) or it is not (whole leading segment becomes path). Evidence: \"a/b://c\" keeps scheme \"a/b\" with host \"c\" and path \"\"; \"a%3A%2F%2Fb\" (percent-encoded) -> no scheme, path is the whole string (no percent-decoding before the search).",
    "confidence": "certain"
   },
   {
    "rule": "No length limit on the scheme was found: a 300-character 'A' run before \"://\" produced a 300-character lowercased scheme and round-tripped through to_string().",
    "confidence": "likely"
   },
   {
    "rule": "Side observation from these probes: is_valid() == (has_host() || has_path()). Scheme presence is irrelevant to validity. Evidence: \"a://\" -> 0; \"abc://\" -> 0; \"://:8080\" -> 0 (port only); \"?://x\" -> 0 (query only); \"#://x\" -> 0 (fragment only); \"http://?q\" -> 0; \"://x\" -> 1 (host only); \":///x\" -> 1 (path only); \"s:// ?q\" -> 1 (host is a single space).",
    "confidence": "likely"
   },
   {
    "rule": "A newline inside argv ends up inside the scheme, so the program then prints MORE than 18 physical lines (the scheme field itself spans two lines). Evidence: $'ht\\ntp://x' -> scheme \"ht\\ntp\". Any consumer that assumes exactly 18 lines must treat fields as \\n-delimited-with-embedded-newlines only if it knows the input.",
    "confidence": "certain"
   }
  ],
