   "Multi-byte UTF-8 was probed only inside a path (\"http://h.com/unicode-bytes\", matched the byte-level model); I did not probe multi-byte bytes as a scheme or inside a port. Byte-level (not codepoint-level) handling is assumed.",
   "Inputs containing a NUL byte are unreachable through argv, so behavior on embedded NUL is untestable via this harness."
  ]
 }
]

OPEN AMBIGUITIES to resolve empirically (run the binary; do not speculate):
1. [Scheme detection and validation] Two formulations are behaviorally IDENTICAL across all 125 probes and I could not construct a discriminating input: (A) split fragment at first '#', then query at first '?' of the remainder, then look for "://" in what is left; (B) find the first "://" in the whole string and reject the scheme if a '?' or '#' occurs before it, then split. Implement either; (A) is simpler and also explains why '?' and '#' terminate the authority.
2. [Scheme detection and validation] Relatedly, I cannot tell whether authority parsing stops at '/', '?', '#' natively or only at '/' because '?'/'#' were already stripped. "http://a#b:80" (host "a", fragment "b:80") is consistent with both.
3. [Scheme detection and validation] to_string() dropping the whole authority when the scheme is empty ("://u@h:1/p?q#f" -> "/p?q#f") is surprising and lossy. It is consistent with 'authority is only concatenated inside the has_scheme branch', but I cannot rule out a different internal shape that happens to agree on every probe.
4. [Scheme detection and validation] No upper bound on scheme length was found (tested 300 chars). A limit above 300 cannot be excluded.
5. [Scheme detection and validation] Case folding was only observed under the default C locale. tolower() behaviour under other locales (e.g. Turkish dotless i) is untestable through argv alone; bytes >= 0x80 were left unchanged here.
6. [Scheme detection and validation] A newline inside the scheme makes the program emit more than 18 physical lines, so 'exactly 18 lines' is not strictly true for inputs containing '\n'.
7. [Scheme detection and validation] Whether an empty scheme (input starting with "://") is stored as an empty string with a separate 'found' flag, or whether has_scheme is derived purely from emptiness, cannot be distinguished; both predict identical output for every probe I ran.
8. [USERINFO SPLITTING inside the authority] to_string()/authority() are lossy in several userinfo cases and this is worth explicit test coverage: "http://a@b:80@c" -> "http://a@b:80" (the "@c" is destroyed), "http://u@[::1]:80" -> "http://u@[" (address destroyed), "http://a@:/p" -> "http://a@/p" (colon destroyed), "http://a@b://c" -> "http://a@b//c". An implementation that keeps the raw authority substring will disagree on all of these.
9. [USERINFO SPLITTING inside the authority] Surprising asymmetry: has_authority() is 0 whenever host is empty, even though authority() returns a non-empty string like "user@" or "user@:8080" and has_userinfo()/has_port() are 1. I could not construct any case where has_authority differs from has_host, so I model has_authority() == !host.empty() — but I cannot rule out that the real predicate is something like "host non-empty" computed at parse time from a separate flag.
10. [USERINFO SPLITTING inside the authority] is_valid() == (!host.empty() || !path.empty()) fits all 89 probes, including the counter-intuitive pair "http://a@?q" -> 0 vs "http://a@/p" -> 1, and "http://u@:0" -> 0 despite has_port 1. It is possible the real implementation is (has_authority() || has_path()) which is observationally identical given the previous point.
11. [USERINFO SPLITTING inside the authority] Whether the port range check is inclusive of 65535 was not re-derived here; I only confirmed 65536 and ":65536" after a userinfo yield absent port ("http://u@:65536" -> port -1, authority "u@").
12. [USERINFO SPLITTING inside the authority] "u@http://h" produces scheme "u@http" — the scheme scan does not validate scheme characters at all, so an '@' can end up inside scheme(). Whether scheme lowercasing would also fold non-ASCII in that position was not probed.
13. [USERINFO SPLITTING inside the authority] stoi-prefix behavior means a port field can consume and discard arbitrary trailing bytes inside the authority (including further '@'). I verified prefix behavior for "80@c", "80:90", "80@", but not for exotic forms like "0x50" or leading-tab port after a userinfo.
14. [HOST / PORT SPLITTING inside the authority (authority reconstruction, has_authority/has_host, IPv6, empty-host cases)] Cannot distinguish two implementations that are behaviorally identical here: (a) the authority substring is cut at the first of '/','?','#' and then fragment/query are split from the remainder, versus (b) the fragment is split at the first '#' and the query at the first '?' globally, and only then is the authority cut at the first '/'. Every probe I tried ("http://a#b:80/c", "http://a#b#c", "http://a?b/c", "http://a:b#c@d", "http://host?q:80") gives the same output under both models. Implement either; I found no discriminating input.
15. [HOST / PORT SPLITTING inside the authority (authority reconstruction, has_authority/has_host, IPv6, empty-host cases)] has_authority() is indistinguishable from has_host() in all 104 probes. It could be implemented as !host.empty(), or as a separate flag that happens to coincide. If the real implementation stores a distinct 'authority present' flag, no probe I ran exposes a difference — including cases where authority() renders non-empty text ("u@", ":8080", ":@:80") while has_authority is 0.
16. [HOST / PORT SPLITTING inside the authority (authority reconstruction, has_authority/has_host, IPv6, empty-host cases)] Whether the port range check is exactly [0,65535] or is 'parse as int then reject if <0 or >65535' cannot be fully separated: "http://host:65536" and "http://host:2147483648" both give -1, so an overflow-throw and a range-reject are indistinguishable at 2147483648. 65535 accepted / 65536 rejected pins the upper bound; "-1" rejected pins the lower bound at >= 0 (0 is accepted).
17. [HOST / PORT SPLITTING inside the authority (authority reconstruction, has_authority/has_host, IPv6, empty-host cases)] Round-trip is lossy in ways that may be surprising: to_string() is NOT byte-identical to the input whenever the port candidate had extra text ("http://a:80:90" -> "http://a:80"), had leading zeros ("http://host:080" -> "http://host:80"), was unparseable ("http://[::1]:8080/" -> "http://[/"), or when the authority contained a bare ':' or '@' ("http://:/path" -> "http:///path", "http://@host" -> "http://host"). I could not determine whether this is intentional normalization or just a side effect of reconstruction.
18. [HOST / PORT SPLITTING inside the authority (authority reconstruction, has_authority/has_host, IPv6, empty-host cases)] "http://[::1]:8080/" and "http://[::1]/" produce byte-identical 18-line output, so IPv6 URLs with and without a port are indistinguishable through this API. Any downstream consumer cannot recover the port. Flagging as almost certainly a bug in the library rather than a deliberate rule, but it must be replicated for byte-equality.
19. [PORT NUMERIC PARSING (host:port split + std::stoi-style parse + range predicate)] std::stoi vs std::stol/stoll/strtol is behaviorally indistinguishable here: every value that would overflow int (>2147483647) also exceeds 65535, so both paths yield 'absent'. Likewise std::stoul cannot be ruled out: stoul("-0")==0 (accepted, matches) and stoul("-80") wraps to a huge value (>65535, rejected, also matches). Implementing as 'optional ws, optional sign, digits, accept iff 0<=signed value<=65535' reproduces all observed outputs.
20. [PORT NUMERIC PARSING (host:port split + std::stoi-style parse + range predicate)] Whether the range test is written as (v < 0 || v > 65535) or (v >= 0 && v <= 65535) or against an unsigned 16-bit cap is unobservable; 65535 accepted / 65536 rejected is the only boundary evidence and it is unambiguous.
21. [PORT NUMERIC PARSING (host:port split + std::stoi-style parse + range predicate)] has_port() could be implemented as port != -1 or port >= 0; since a successful parse can never produce a negative value, the two are indistinguishable.
22. [PORT NUMERIC PARSING (host:port split + std::stoi-style parse + range predicate)] isspace() is locale-dependent in principle. All six C-locale whitespace bytes are skipped; NBSP (C2 A0) is not. I could not test under a non-C locale, so a locale-sensitive difference cannot be fully excluded (the binary presumably never calls setlocale, so the classic 'C' locale set is the safe implementation).
23. [PORT NUMERIC PARSING (host:port split + std::stoi-style parse + range predicate)] A literal NUL byte inside the port text could not be tested (bash strips it from argv), so behavior of an embedded '\0' is unknown — likely unreachable via argv anyway.
24. [PORT NUMERIC PARSING (host:port split + std::stoi-style parse + range predicate)] SURPRISING (worth a regression test): "http://h:-0" yields port 0 with has_port 1, i.e. a negative-signed input is accepted. Also surprising: "http://h:8?0" -> port 8 (not 80) because the authority terminator is found before the numeric parse.
25. [PORT NUMERIC PARSING (host:port split + std::stoi-style parse + range predicate)] SURPRISING: a rejected port silently deletes the ':'+text, so to_string() is lossy and NOT round-trip stable for inputs like "http://h:abc" -> "http://h" and "http://h:65536" -> "http://h".
26. [PATH / QUERY / FRAGMENT SPLITTING] SURPRISING (but firmly established): the '#'/'?' split runs over the RAW input before the "://" scheme search. This means a '#' or '?' occurring anywhere before the "://" destroys scheme AND authority parsing entirely ("http#://ex.com" has no scheme and no host). Any reimplementation that parses the scheme first and only then splits query/fragment will diverge on these inputs. This is the single highest-risk rule in this family.
27. [PATH / QUERY / FRAGMENT SPLITTING] SURPRISING: to_string() emits the authority only when has_scheme() is true, so "://ex.com" round-trips to the empty string even though host()/authority() are populated and is_valid() is 1. I could not find any input with an empty scheme whose to_string included the authority.
28. [PATH / QUERY / FRAGMENT SPLITTING] to_string() is not injective: "http://ex.com", "http://ex.com?", "http://ex.com#", and "http://ex.com?#" all produce to_string "http://ex.com". Likewise "http://ex.com/?#?#" -> "http://ex.com/#?#". So an empty-but-present query/fragment is unrecoverable.
29. [PATH / QUERY / FRAGMENT SPLITTING] is_valid() == has_host() || has_path() fits all 92 probes, but I could not construct a case that separates it from a possible alternative like "!(host.empty() && path.empty())" plus some scheme condition — every scheme-bearing input I tried with empty host and empty path ("http://", "a://", "http://#ex.com", "http://?ex.com") returned 0, and every input with a non-empty host or path returned 1. Marked 'likely' rather than 'certain' only because the rule is inferred from coincidence across cases rather than from an isolating probe.
30. [PATH / QUERY / FRAGMENT SPLITTING] has_authority() tracked has_host() exactly in all probes; I never found an input where they differed. It is possible the implementation checks something subtly different (e.g. "host non-empty" vs "authority region contained a host"), but no probe distinguishes them.
31. [PATH / QUERY / FRAGMENT SPLITTING] I did not test bytes above 0x7F (UTF-8 / raw high bytes) or embedded NUL in the path/query/fragment; NUL cannot be passed through argv at all, and multi-byte behavior is presumed byte-verbatim by analogy with tabs and spaces but was not directly probed.
32. [PATH / QUERY / FRAGMENT SPLITTING] Very long inputs (length-based truncation or overflow in the splitting code) were not probed.
33. [is_valid() truth table — exact predicate] `has_host() || has_path()` and `has_authority() || has_path()` are OBSERVATIONALLY IDENTICAL: has_authority() == !host().empty() in every one of ~7000 probes (I fuzz-checked this equality explicitly: 0 divergences). I could not construct any input where authority-presence and host-non-emptiness differ, so which one is_valid() internally consults is undeterminable. Implementing either gives byte-identical output.
34. [is_valid() truth table — exact predicate] Likewise `!path_.empty()` vs `has_path()` cannot be distinguished, since has_path() == !path().empty() held on all probes (including path ' ', path tab, path 0x01).
35. [is_valid() truth table — exact predicate] Whether is_valid() is a stored flag computed at parse time or recomputed on each call is unobservable from a single-shot CLI (each run parses once).
36. [is_valid() truth table — exact predicate] Surprising (outside my family but load-bearing for anyone reimplementing): to_string() for '://x' is the EMPTY string even though host is 'x' and is_valid is 1; and to_string() for '://h/p?q#f' is '/p?q#f' — the authority is dropped from to_string when has_scheme is false. This is why is_valid must NOT be derived from to_string.
37. [is_valid() truth table — exact predicate] I found no input at all where the predicate fails. If a counterexample exists it must live outside: all strings of length <=4 over {a,:,/,?,#,@,space}, all strings of length 4 over {h,8,:,/,@,#}, 6000 random token-concatenations over a 33-token alphabet, and ~180 hand-chosen probes (long strings, non-UTF8 bytes, control chars, embedded newline, IPv6-ish brackets, percent-encodings).
38. [to_string() reconstruction / serialization] Probe-count accounting: ~51,200 combinatorial round-trip comparisons (scheme x userinfo x host x port x path x query x fragment sweep, 46,592 of them lossy) plus a 2,599-input randomized varied corpus, plus a 1,200-input sample of the hardest known-lossy inputs, plus ~100 hand-crafted probes. Distinct input strings is roughly 40,000 after dedup. The full serialization model was machine-checked against 3,839 of those with 0 mismatches; the rest were only checked for round-trip equality.
39. [to_string() reconstruction / serialization] Whether to_string() literally calls authority() or re-derives userinfo/host/port inline is not observable from the outside: every probe produced a to_string authority slice byte-identical to the printed authority() field, including all degenerate combinations. Implementing it either way is safe.
40. [to_string() reconstruction / serialization] Whether the scheme gate is `!scheme_.empty()` or a separate boolean `has_scheme_` member is not distinguishable, because I found no input where the scheme string is empty but a scheme was 'found' in a way that produced any output difference — '://x' consistently emits nothing for the scheme+authority portion. Both implementations behave identically on everything I probed.
41. [to_string() reconstruction / serialization] Port acceptance boundary: I confirmed 0, 65534 and 65535 accepted and 65536, 2147483647, 99999999999999 and -80 rejected, so the check is almost certainly 0 <= p <= 65535. I did not probe every value in between, and I did not determine whether the rejection of huge values comes from a std::out_of_range catch or from the explicit range check (both end in the same observable behavior: the whole ':port' is omitted).
42. [to_string() reconstruction / serialization] '-0' being accepted as port 0 (emitted as ':0') while '-80' is rejected is genuinely surprising and worth a dedicated regression test; it implies the sign is consumed by the integer parse and only the resulting VALUE is range-checked, rather than the port string being screened for a leading '-'.
43. [to_string() reconstruction / serialization] Exact ordering of scheme-detection vs the '#'/'?' splits: my evidence ('ht#tp://h', 'ht?tp://h', 'a?b://c' all yield no scheme, while '/a://b', 'a/b://c', 'a b://c', ':://a' all yield a scheme) is equally consistent with (a) splitting fragment then query first and only then searching the remainder for '://', or (b) searching for '://' first and rejecting the candidate if it contains '#' or '?'. I could not construct an input that separates the two models. This matters for to_string only because it decides whether the authority is emitted at all.
44. [to_string() reconstruction / serialization] Inputs containing a literal LF break the '18 lines of output' contract: to_string() re-emits the newline verbatim, so 'http://h/a\nb' prints 20 lines. Any differential-testing harness must compare raw bytes or split on a known field count rather than assuming 18 lines.
45. [to_string() reconstruction / serialization] to_string() is idempotent as a string transform (0 violations in 2,599 tries) but is NOT information-preserving: for '://h' the first parse has host()=='h' while the reparse of its to_string ('') has no host. So to_string cannot be used as a canonical key for parse-equivalence.
46. [HOSTILE / EXOTIC INPUTS — bytes, whitespace, size, and argv handling] The 'exactly 18 lines' contract is only true byte-wise for newline-free input. Because raw '\n' bytes in argv[1] are copied into fields verbatim, an input containing k newlines produces 18 records but 18+k*(number of fields containing them) newline bytes (e.g. "http://h/p\nq" -> 20 newlines, since the \n appears in both path and to_string). A reimplementation must copy bytes verbatim rather than sanitize.
47. [HOSTILE / EXOTIC INPUTS — bytes, whitespace, size, and argv handling] Scheme lowercasing was only shown to affect ASCII A-Z; bytes 0xC0, 0xDE, 0xC3, 0x9C, 0x80, 0xFF were left unchanged. This is consistent with ::tolower under the C locale, but a locale-sensitive build/environment (e.g. LC_ALL=de_DE.ISO-8859-1) could in principle map single-byte Latin-1 uppercase letters. I only tested the default environment locale, so 'ASCII-only' is certain for this environment but the mechanism (tolower vs explicit 'A'<=c<='Z') is not distinguishable behaviorally here.
48. [HOSTILE / EXOTIC INPUTS — bytes, whitespace, size, and argv handling] to_string() is lossy/asymmetric in three ways that look like bugs rather than design: (a) when the scheme is empty but '://' was present, the authority is silently dropped ("://h:80/p" -> "/p"), so to_string is not a round-trip; (b) when has_scheme is true the authority is emitted even when has_authority() is false ("http://u@:80/p"); (c) '?' / '#' with empty content are dropped. It is unclear which of these the reference implementation intends, but all three are reproducible.
49. [HOSTILE / EXOTIC INPUTS — bytes, whitespace, size, and argv handling] IPv6 literals are simply broken ("http://[::1]:8080/p" -> host "["), and is_valid() still reports 1. There is no bracket handling whatsoever, so it is ambiguous whether a reimplementation is expected to reproduce this corruption — for byte-equality it must.
50. [HOSTILE / EXOTIC INPUTS — bytes, whitespace, size, and argv handling] is_valid() ignores the scheme entirely, so "a://" is invalid while the plain text "a" would be valid; and a fragment-only or query-only input is invalid while a whitespace-only input is valid. Surprising but consistent across every probe: is_valid == (host non-empty || path non-empty).
51. [HOSTILE / EXOTIC INPUTS — bytes, whitespace, size, and argv handling] The 131072-byte failure is an OS execve limit (MAX_ARG_STRLEN), so I could not determine whether the library has any internal size limit above 128 KiB. No limit was observed up to 131070 bytes.
52. [HOSTILE / EXOTIC INPUTS — bytes, whitespace, size, and argv handling] I did not manage to distinguish 'authority ends at first of /?#' from 'authority ends at first /' because '?' and '#' are already removed before the authority is parsed; both models predict every observed output. Implementing either is safe.
53. [HOSTILE / EXOTIC INPUTS — bytes, whitespace, size, and argv handling] NUL bytes cannot be passed through argv, so behavior on embedded NUL is untestable and unknown.
54. [Systematic structural combination sweep (scheme x userinfo x host x port x path x query x fragment cartesian products)] has_authority() is observationally identical to has_host() on all 6,900 probes, so I cannot distinguish 'has_authority == !host.empty()' from 'has_authority == (saw "://" && !host.empty())'. Both models fit every observation, because host can only be non-empty when "://" was found. Either is safe to implement.
55. [Systematic structural combination sweep (scheme x userinfo x host x port x path x query x fragment cartesian products)] Likewise is_valid() == (has_host() || has_path()) is indistinguishable from (has_authority() || has_path()) for the same reason. No probe separates them.
56. [Systematic structural combination sweep (scheme x userinfo x host x port x path x query x fragment cartesian products)] Port rejection cannot be attributed to a specific mechanism: a std::stoi std::out_of_range throw (":9999999999999999999999", ":2147483648") and an explicit post-parse range check (":65536") produce identical output (port -1, dropped from authority() and to_string()). ":-80"/":-1" likewise cannot distinguish 'negative rejected by range check' from 'sign not accepted'.
57. [Systematic structural combination sweep (scheme x userinfo x host x port x path x query x fragment cartesian products)] The relative order of the authority/path split versus the query/fragment split is not observable: 'strip fragment, strip query, then find the first / in the remainder' and 'end authority at the first of / ? #' are provably equivalent on all inputs and both matched every probe. I chose the former in the stated algorithm.
58. [Systematic structural combination sweep (scheme x userinfo x host x port x path x query x fragment cartesian products)] The to_string() behavior for an empty scheme ("://h.com" -> to_string "") looks like an unintentional bug rather than a designed rule, but it is 100% reproducible across the whole "://" arm of the sweep. Any reimplementation judged byte-for-byte against the binary MUST replicate it.
59. [Systematic structural combination sweep (scheme x userinfo x host x port x path x query x fragment cartesian products)] std::stoi's whitespace skipping was probed only with ASCII spaces (":  +65535xyz"); I did not probe a tab/\v/\f immediately after the ':' in a port, so locale/isspace details there are inferred rather than observed.
60. [Systematic structural combination sweep (scheme x userinfo x host x port x path x query x fragment cartesian products)] Multi-byte UTF-8 was probed only inside a path ("http://h.com/unicode-bytes", matched the byte-level model); I did not probe multi-byte bytes as a scheme or inside a port. Byte-level (not codepoint-level) handling is assumed.
61. [Systematic structural combination sweep (scheme x userinfo x host x port x path x query x fragment cartesian products)] Inputs containing a NUL byte are unreachable through argv, so behavior on embedded NUL is untestable via this harness.

Design targeted probes that discriminate between the competing explanations for each ambiguity. Run at least 40 probes. For each ambiguity, report the resolved rule and the evidence that settles it. If an ambiguity cannot be settled, say exactly why and what the residual risk is.

